ADD += [
    ('04', 'APG-S04-L0175', 'APG-S04-DS028', 'bullet', 'Bring the record kinds checked today only inside the calculations under this section\'s intake; take a regression baseline of today\'s rule and figure results first, explain and sign off every difference, and do it under its own change request. The records written straight to the store today and the in-code authority register enter the intake as new record kinds, without waiting for that move.', 'C02'),
    ('04', 'APG-S04-L0175', 'APG-S04-DS029', 'bullet', 'Keep the controlled data-supply registers (entity, field, rule input contracts, field coverage, supply routes, messages) current, and regenerate the field-coverage report for each way in as it is built; the report fails on a gap. The report rests on a scan of the code, which is not a proof: each rule\'s inputs are also confirmed by that rule\'s tests when the rule is next changed.', 'C06, C21'),
    ('04', 'APG-S04-C0188', 'APG-S04-DS030', 'amendment record', REC, 'C21'),
    # ---- Section 05: calculation engine
    ('05', 'APG-S05-L0034', 'APG-S05-DS001', 'bullet', 'Show the approved budget (§3.3) and the authoritative forecast\'s final cost and final margin (§5.1) first, the forecast figures labelled as forecasts (Section 03 §5). Beside them, show each item that is not final in its own column, mapped to the locked states: awaiting approval (on the revenue side, Section 11 §8 draft or submitted values and submitted or unresolved claims; on the cost side, purchase orders and subcontracts not yet approved (Section 03 §13: draft or submitted, so not yet commitments, Section 03 §4) and budget revisions not yet approved (§3.3: approved changes)); on hold (records pending reconciliation or quarantined); disputed and rejected (both Section 11\'s one "Disputed/rejected amount" state, in two columns by its recorded status; budget revisions are mapped to §3.3\'s approved changes, as they have no state of their own); each with its amount and the effect it would have on the budget, the forecast cost or the margin if it went through; for a cost item, the effect on forecast cost is only the part not already represented in the forecast cost to complete (above). Potential, estimated and speculative values are not shown in these columns: they stay analytical opportunities (Section 11 §4.1). Items the builder records as carried at his own cost or withdrawn sit in the rejected column, with no what-if. These effects are what-if values: labelled, isolated as Section 10 §7.1 requires, and never added into the authoritative figure. For held records they follow Section 04 §9\'s rules for provisional views: shown to authorised reviewers and traceable to the unverified records.', 'C14'),
    ('05', 'APG-S05-L0034', 'APG-S05-DS002', 'bullet', 'The extras and variations view (Section 11 §8.2) is a breakdown of the authoritative forecast final margin, not a separate margin: for each instruction or variation, its approved revenue and its share of forecast final cost under the decomposition above (cost recognised to date plus forecast cost to complete, with an attributed commitment counted once, inside the forecast cost to complete); the original scope is the remainder. Amounts are in whole cents and the original scope is the remainder, so the parts add up exactly to the authoritative figure; any percentage shown follows §12. The view is labelled as a breakdown with its definition (Section 03 §5.4). Items whose approval or commercial state is not final are shown beside it as above, never in it.', 'C13'),
    ('05', ('p', 36), 'APG-S05-DS006', 'paragraph', 'The builder chooses how the approved budget is broken down: by stage, by trade or cost code, by area, or a mix (the work packages and cost codes of Section 03 §2). The parts add up to the whole-job approved budget, which is always kept; where they do not, Profit Guardian says so and by how much, and a figure for a part is not shown as complete. The breakdown is taken from the builder\'s own system or file where it holds one, and the builder is asked only for what is missing. Where the builder breaks it down in more than one way (for example by stage and by trade), each breakdown adds up to the whole-job approved budget on its own. The structure of the breakdown (which parts exist and how costs map to them) is a setting, approved as other settings are (Section 04 §18.3); the amount of each part is part of the approved budget baseline (§3.3) and is approved as the budget and its changes are. Under the one-person exception (Section 04 §18.3), that person approves both, marked.', 'C11'),
    ('05', 'APG-S05-L0089', 'APG-S05-DS003', 'bullet', 'Where a cost is attributed to an instruction or variation, it stays exactly once in cost recognised to date or in forecast cost to complete; the attribution groups it and never adds it a second time. A cost, or forecast cost to complete, that is not attributed stays with the original scope.', 'C13'),
    ('05', 'APG-S05-C0176', 'APG-S05-DS004', 'amendment record', REC, 'C21'),
    # ---- Section 06: intelligence
    ('06', 'APG-S06-C0040', 'APG-S06-DS001', 'paragraph', 'Rule thresholds and windows ship with defaults approved by the product owner (the financial severity levels of §8 are not among them: they have no default, and the builder sets them, DS002); the builder\'s authorised approver accepts them at onboarding and may adjust them within the approved ranges. Accepting a default is an approval of the builder\'s own setting (Section 04 §18.3): where the builder has exactly one authorised person, he accepts it himself, and it carries the marker "approved by the person who entered it". Each default is documented and visible to the builder; nothing is applied silently (Section 04 §10). A setting with no sensible default (the builder\'s own categories, or pairs a person declares) is supplied by the builder when the rule is switched on.', 'C10'),
    ('06', 'APG-S06-C0087', 'APG-S06-DS002', 'paragraph', 'The financial part of a finding\'s severity is graded against levels the builder sets at setup: for each grade an amount, a percentage, or both; the grade applies as soon as any level set for it is reached. The builder chooses what a percentage is measured against: the whole job, the part of the job the finding sits in (a stage, trade or area of his budget breakdown, Section 05 §3.3), or both. For the whole job, the percentage is of the job\'s approved budget; for a part, of that part\'s approved budget (Section 05 §3.3). A finding that sits in more than one part (a stage and a trade) is measured against each; the more serious grade applies, and the finding says which part triggered it. A finding not tied to a part, or a cost not tagged to one, is graded on the amount and the whole job only, and says so. Where a value a percentage needs is missing, only the levels that can be measured apply and the finding says so; a grade with only percentage levels then does not apply. No default or suggested levels are supplied. The levels are approved as other settings are (Section 04 §18.3). Until they are set and approved, findings still show; their money grade is marked ungraded, with what to do, and their non-financial classification still applies. A finding\'s safety, quality, contractual and compliance classification is assessed on its own and is never lowered by a small or missing amount: for quality findings by Section 13 §13.4, and for other findings by an approved, documented scheme (§8: documented, testable and approved), to be defined before it is built. A finding with no defensible amount is shown with that classification and marked as having no amount.', 'C11'),
    ('06', ('p', 111), 'APG-S06-DS006', 'paragraph', 'Profit Guardian advises; it does not block. A finding always shows, graded on its own seriousness (§8). The builder decides what to do: a person whose role permits it (role-based permissions, Section 15 §15.4) may ignore (dismiss) any finding in one tap; quick reasons are offered and may be skipped. The dismissal is recorded with who dismissed it and when (PG-AI-AC-13); the finding and its evidence stay on record and can be reopened; it is raised again only when something material changes, under §9.1\'s re-notification rules. A deferment is recorded in the same way; a deferred finding is raised again on the date the person chose (§6: next review date), or sooner if something material changes. Dismissing or deferring a finding never closes, verifies or changes the record it is about (Section 13 §13.8). Acceptance criterion PG-DS-AC-16 tests this (it is kept with this change request\'s other criteria in Section 04 §19).', 'C22'),
    ('06', 'APG-S06-C0173', 'APG-S06-DS003', 'amendment record', REC6, 'C21'),
    # ---- Section 11: commercial
    ('11', ('p', 52), 'APG-S11-DS001', 'paragraph', 'For a rejected variation, the builder records what happens next: disputed, carried at the builder\'s own cost, resubmitted, or withdrawn. Disputed, own cost and withdrawn (the builder abandons the claim: nil value, history kept) are recorded statuses of the one Disputed/rejected state, not new states. A resubmission is a change of state back to submitted, made through the process §8.1 requires, with its own record. Profit Guardian records the outcome and does not judge the dispute (§11); the cost already incurred stays visible.', 'C13'),
    ('11', 'APG-S11-C0055', 'APG-S11-DS002', 'heading 2', '8.2 Extras and variations view — mandatory', 'C13'),
    ('11', 'APG-S11-C0055', 'APG-S11-DS003', 'paragraph', 'Show each extra or variation separately from the original contract scope: its value in each state, its status, the cost attributed to it so far, and its effect on the job\'s profit, as the breakdown Section 05 §3.2 defines. The original scope and the extras are never blended, and no state is added into another.', 'C13'),
    ('11', 'APG-S11-C0112', 'APG-S11-DS004', 'amendment record', REC, 'C21'),
]
